{
 "turns_text": [
  "[SEG0001] person_1 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Sorry we're late, doctor. The car park was a nightmare and Dad wouldn't put his coat on.",
  "[SEG0002] person_2 (DOCTOR): Oh, don't apologise, you're here now. Come and sit down. Bit of a grey morning, isn't it.",
  "[SEG0003] person_3 (PATIENT): I'm tired. Can we just get on with it.",
  "[SEG0004] person_1 (FAMILY_MEMBER): He's been grumpy since he got up, if I'm honest.",
  "[SEG0005] person_2 (DOCTOR): Sounds like a rough start. What did you want to talk about today?",
  "[SEG0006] person_3 (PATIENT): These mornings. I wake up sweating and shaky. It's doing my head in.",
  "[SEG0007] person_2 (DOCTOR): I'm sorry, that does sound rotten. How long has it been going on?",
  "[SEG0008] person_3 (PATIENT): Um, three weeks. No, hang on, nearer five weeks. Most mornings, before I'm even out of bed.",
  "[SEG0009] person_2 (DOCTOR): And what do you do when it hits you?",
  "[SEG0010] person_3 (PATIENT): Nothing much. I skip breakfast, can't face it. If I force some toast down later it eases off.",
  "[SEG0011] person_1 (FAMILY_MEMBER): There was one morning as well, I couldn't tell you when exactly, he was confused. Couldn't find the kettle, kept asking me what day it was.",
  "[SEG0012] person_3 (PATIENT): Yeah, all right. That one time I was all over the place. It passed once I'd sat down.",
  "[SEG0013] person_2 (DOCTOR): Okay. Talk me through the sugar tablets you're actually taking.",
  "[SEG0014] person_3 (PATIENT): The big white ones. Metforman, I always mash that name up. Uh, five hundred milligrams... no, a thousand. A thousand milligrams, twice a day.",
  "[SEG0015] person_3 (PATIENT): And the little sugar tablet, Diamicron. Err, I nearly said eighty milligrams. It's forty. Forty milligrams in the morning.",
  "[SEG0016] person_2 (DOCTOR): Right, so Diamicron is the gliclazide, forty milligrams each morning, and the metformin a thousand twice daily.",
  "[SEG0017] person_1 (FAMILY_MEMBER): He doesn't take the tablets on Sundays, though. I've watched him leave them in the pot.",
  "[SEG0018] person_3 (PATIENT): Not Sundays. It's Saturdays. I have a lie-in and I forget the morning ones.",
  "[SEG0019] person_2 (DOCTOR): So it's Saturdays the morning tablets get missed. Any cuts or sores that have been slow to heal?",
  "[SEG0020] person_1 (FAMILY_MEMBER): He caught his shin on the garden gate. That cut healed in five days, completely ordinary.",
  "[SEG0021] person_3 (PATIENT): Yeah. Five days. It was nothing.",
  "[SEG0022] person_3 (PATIENT): My meter said three point one this morning, when I was shaking.",
  "[SEG0023] person_4 (NURSE): Sorry to butt in. He showed me that reading in the corridor. Capillary glucose three point one millimoles.",
  "[SEG0024] person_2 (DOCTOR): Thanks, that's really helpful. You're not sweaty sitting here, but your hands are a little shaky, and your pulse feels regular. Any chest pain with these spells?",
  "[SEG0025] person_3 (PATIENT): No. Nothing like that.",
  "[SEG0026] person_2 (DOCTOR): And your breathing, any trouble there?",
  "[SEG0027] person_3 (PATIENT): No. Breathing's fine.",
  "[SEG0028] person_2 (DOCTOR): How long have you had the diabetes?",
  "[SEG0029] person_3 (PATIENT): Twelve years. Type two. Sick of it, frankly.",
  "[SEG0030] person_2 (DOCTOR): I hear you. My working impression is these morning spells are low blood sugars, from the gliclazide on an empty stomach. I won't be certain until the bloods are back, but that's what I think is going on.",
  "[SEG0031] person_2 (DOCTOR): I'd like you to stop skipping breakfast and take the morning tablets with some food, even just toast, and I'll keep the doses as they are. I'll arrange a blood test, HbA1c, kidney function and a glucose, and reception can book it this week. Come back and see me in a fortnight with the results. If you get confused again, or the shakes don't settle once you're eating, ring us rather than waiting.",
  "[SEG0032] person_1 (FAMILY_MEMBER): I'll make sure he eats before the morning ones. Come on, Dad."
 ],
